   Replace placeholders' values accordingly.

These are the basic steps required to create and deploy your own Smart Contracts for Defi projects. Note that there are several other intricacies involved, like handling user roles, ensuring proper authorization for the token owner/minter, writing unit tests, and optimizing/gas optimization, among others. Therefore, it's best practice to seek professional advice or collaborate with developers experienced in DeFi token creation to ensure optimal performance and security. As for the recommended toolset to facilitate the creation, deployment, and testing of your contracts, Truffle, Hardhat, Remix, Mocha, Chai are commonly used IDE/development environments, testing frameworks, libraries, and tools in the Ethereum ecosystem.

TypeScript is a strongly-typed superset of JavaScript that provides additional features such as type annotations, interfaces, classes, modules, etc. It compiles to plain JavaScript so it can be run in any modern browser or node.js environment.

The main benefit of TypeScript is that it enables developers to catch errors early on during development before running their code, resulting in fewer bugs and more maintainable code. Type safety helps to avoid common mistakes related to null/undefined values, incorrect argument types, inconsistent data structures across functions, and other issues that can arise with pure JavaScript.

Duck typing is a concept in object-oriented programming languages like TypeScript that refers to the practice of checking an object's behavior rather than its type. In other words, objects that behave similarly, regardless of their actual type, will be treated as if they had the same underlying structure. This means that a function or method can accept any object instance whose behavior matches what's expected.

For example, let's say we have a method `displayInfo(obj)` which expects an object containing name and age properties:

```
function displayInfo(obj: {name: string; age: number}): void {
  console.log(`My name is ${obj.name} and I am ${obj.age}`);
}
```

We could pass any object that has both a `name` property of type `string` and an `age` property of type `number`, even though they may not necessarily have the same origin or be part of the same class hierarchy. Here's an example:

```typescript
interface Person {
    name: string;
    age: number;
}

class Student implements Person {
    public name: string;
    public age: number;

    constructor(name: string, age: number) {
      this.name = name;
      this.age = age;
    }

    study(): void {
        console.log(`${this.name} is studying`);
    }
}

class Employee implements Person {
    public name: string;
    public age: number;

    constructor(name: string, age: number) {
      this.name = name;
      this.age = age;
    }

    work(): void{
        console.log(`${this.name} is working.`);
    }
}

const student: Student = new Student("Alice", 21);
const employee: Employee = new Employee("Bob", 35);

displayInfo(student); // Expected output: My name is Alice and I am 21
displayInfo(employee); // Expected output: My name is Bob and I am 35

// Passing an object without implementing Person interface
const wrongObj = {name: 'Charlie', age: 25};

try {
  displayInfo(wrongObj);
} catch (error) {
  console.error(error.message);
}

/* Output:
Argument of type '{ name: string; age: number; }' is not assignable to parameter of type '{ name: string; age: number; }'.
  Types of property 'name' are incompatible.
    Type'string' is missing the following properties from type'string': Length, FindIndex(accept: Exclude<...>, fromIndex?: number), charAt(...),... */
*/
```

As you can see, we were able to use the `Student` and `Employee` instances without explicitly defining them using TypeScript's duck typing capability, saving time by avoiding lengthy code repetition, and making our program more adaptable and resilient. At the same time, we avoided possible runtime errors by using TypeScript to enforce type correctness throughout the program.

Here is an example implementation of a discord.js v14 bot that only handles the `ban` command:

```TypeScript
// Import required modules
import { Client, IntentsBitField } from 'discord.js';

const client = new Client({
  intents: [
    IntentsBitField.Flags.Guilds,
    IntentsBitField.Flags.GuildMessages,
    IntentsBitField.Flags.MessageContent,
  ],
});

client.on('ready', () => {
  console.log(`Logged in as ${client.user?.tag}`);
});

client.on('guildMessageCreate', async (message) => {
  // Check if message is a valid slash command and whether it's called "ban"
  if (!message.isContextMenu() && message.content.startsWith('/ban')) {
    const args = message.content.split(" ").slice(1);
    const mention = args[0]?? null;

    try {
      // Get the guild object based on message source
      const guild = await client.guilds.fetch(message.channel.guildId);

      // Grab the user or member associated with the provided mention
      let target;
      if (mention) {
        target = await message.guild.members.fetch({ user: mention });
        if (!target) {
          throw Error(`Invalid user or member specified`);
        }
      }

      // Ensure correct permissions to run the /ban command
      if (!message.member.permissions.has("BAN_MEMBERS")) {
        throw Error(`You need the BAN_MEMBERS permission to use this command.`);
      }

      // Ban the user/member based on provided arguments and reply with corresponding message
      if (target) {
        await target.ban();
        return message.reply(`**${target.displayName}** was successfully banned!`);
      } else {
        const users = await message.guild.fetchMembers();
        const username = await message.member.displayName;
        return message.reply(
          `Please provide a valid user or member to ban.\n\nPossible matches:\n- ${
            Object.values(users).filter((u) =>
              u.displayName.toLowerCase().includes(username.toLowerCase())
        ).map((member) => `${member.displayName} (${member.id})`).join('\n- ') || 'None'
          }\n`
        );
      }
    } catch (error) {
      console.error(error);
      return message.reply({ content: 'Something went wrong while banning this user:', ephemeral: true });
    }
  }
});

async function registerCommands(guildID: string) {
  await client.application.commands.set([], guildID);
  await client.application.commands.create({
    name: 'ban',
    description: 'Bans a guild member(s)',
    options: [
      {
        type: 6, // Mentionable option type
        name: 'user_or_member',
        description: 'Member to ban (optional; will ban everyone matching your criteria without it)',
        required: false,
      },
    ],
  }, guildID);
}

(async (): Promise<void> => {
  // Login the bot and fetch relevant data
  await client.login(process.env.DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN);

  // Register the slash command for all available servers
  const servers = await client.guilds.cache.array();
  for (let index = 0; index < servers.length; index++) {
    const server = servers[index];
    await registerCommands(server.id);
  }

  // Listen for guild events
  client.on('interactionCreate', async (interaction) => {
    // Only process interactions coming from the main bot account
    if (interaction.isApplicationCommand()) {
      switch (interaction.commandName) {
        case 'ban':
          break;

        default:
          return interaction.reply({ ephemeral: true, content: 'This command is not yet implemented' });
      }

      // Handle errors by replying and calling `closeModal()` if necessary
      try {
        await interaction.deferReply();
        await interaction.editReply({ content: 'Executing command...' });
        // Your implementation code here!

        await interaction.followUp({ content: 'Done!' });
      } catch (error) {
        console.log(`Error executing ${interaction.commandName}:`, error);
        await interaction.editReply({ content: 'An error occurred.' }).catch(() => {});
      }
    }
  });

  // Login the bot instance
})();

```

