Yes, we can solve this problem using bitwise operations in Java.

Here's one possible approach:

```java
public boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
    // We only need to consider positive integers since negative powers don't exist
    if (n < 0) {
        return false;
    }

    int numZeros = Integer.numberOfLeadingZeros(n);
    int bitPosition = 31 - numZeros;

    // If an integer has more than one bit set or its last bit position is not equal to 30,
    // then it cannot be a power of three
    return (bitPosition == 30 && ((n & -n) == 1))? true : false;
}
```

The algorithm consists of two steps:

1. Get the number of leading zeros and the position of the rightmost bit set in `n`. This will tell us whether `n` is a power of three based on certain conditions.

   - The number of leading zeros indicates how many bits are left un-set before encountering a non-zero bit. If it equals `29`, then all bits except for the one at index `30` are zero, satisfying the condition for being a power of three (the exponent becomes `2`, which is also the count of leading zeros).

   - The position of the rightmost bit set tells us how many bits have been "used" by `n`. For instance, if its value is `20` (meaning the bit at index `21` is set), then this means that `n` has at least two bit positions occupied because `(21 + 1)` would exceed `30`. Therefore, `n` is not a power of three anymore.

2. Check if `n` meets the criteria established in step 1. In particular, it requires that the rightmost bit of `n` is set and that the remaining bits to the left of it are all zeroes:

   ```java
   // If an integer has more than one bit set or its last bit position is not equal to 30,
   // then it cannot be a power of three
   return (bitPosition == 30 && ((n & -n) == 1))? true : false;
   ```

   Here, `-n` computes the single most significant bit in `n` (i.e., the highest-power-of-two factor). We perform a bitwise AND between `n` and `-n`, removing all lower-bit positions from `n` but keeping the highest bit set. Then, `(n & -n) == 1` checks whether the resulting value equals `1`, meaning that all other bits were cleared.

   Additionally, we make sure that `bitPosition` matches `30`, indicating that precisely one bit was active in our calculation. 

Note that we've covered the constraints mentioned earlier (`n >= 0`) as part of the first step, so no extra code is needed for it. Also, this implementation does not rely on any loops or recursion, fulfilling the requirement of the follow-up question.

