Sure, the `if` statement is one of the most common and fundamental control flow statements in any programming language, including C.

An `if` statement in C evaluates a given condition and performs an action depending on whether the condition evaluates to true or false. Here’s the syntax for an `if` statement:

```c
if (condition) {
   // action(s) 
}
```

- `condition`: This is a logical expression that evaluates to either `true` or `false`. It can be any arithmetic expression, variable comparisons, logical operators, etc.
- `action(s)`: These are the statements executed by the compiler when the `condition` is true (i.e., returns non-zero value).

For instance, let's consider a simple example to illustrate the usage and basic structure of an `if` statement:

```c
int x = 10;
int y = 5;

if (x > y) {
    printf("x is greater than y\n"); // This statement will execute only when x is greater than y
}
```
In this example, we first initialize two integer variables `x` and `y`.

Next, we check if the condition `x > y` is `true` using an `if` statement. If the condition holds true, then the statement within the curly braces (`{}`) will get executed, which in our case causes the function `printf()` to display the text "x is greater than y" on the console.

Now, let's take another example involving multiple conditions separated by `else`, and chained together via `elif`/`else-if` statements, respectively:

```c
float mark = 85.0f;

if (mark >= 90.0f) {                  // Condition 1: Mark >= 90%
    printf("Distinction\n");           // Executed when mark >= 90%
} else if (mark >= 80.0f && mark < 90.0f){// Condition 2: 80% <= Mark < 90%
    printf("First Class\n");            // Executed when 80% <= mark < 90%
} else if (mark >= 70.0f && mark < 80.0f){// Condition 3: 70% <= Mark < 80%
    printf("Second Class Upper\n");     // Executed when 70% <= mark < 80%
} else if (mark >= 60.0f && mark < 70.0f){// Condition 4: 60% <= Mark < 70%
    printf("Second Class Lower\n");     // Executed when 60% <= mark < 70%
} else {                                 // Default condition - Marks below 60%
    printf("Fail\n");                     // Executed when mark < 60%
}
```

Here, we use four distinct conditional statements with three intermediate `else-if` statements to evaluate the marks entered by the user.

Each condition is checked sequentially until one of them becomes valid (returns `true`). If none of the preceding conditions satisfies, then the default condition will execute, displaying “Fail”. 

I hope these examples help clarify how to use the `if` statement effectively in your C programs. Good luck with your coding projects!

